---

`import.meta.og.bake(fn)` runs `fn` **at build**, serializes its result, and rewrites the call to that literal. At runtime, there is no function and no work: only the answer.

```ts
import { buildNav } from './nav';

const nav = import.meta.og.bake(() => buildNav());
// becomes, at build:
// const nav = { items: [ … the whole computed tree … ] };
```

This is the "run at build, ship the answer" trick. A nav tree computed from a corpus, a config object assembled from several files, an RSS payload, anything costly and unchanging: compute it one time when you build, not on each request, and not in the browser.

## How it runs

At build, the macro bundles `fn` together with the imports that it uses. It executes the bundle in Node, serializes the result with [devalue](https://github.com/Rich-Harris/devalue), and inlines it. devalue is the serializer. Thus the result can be far more than JSON: `Date`, `Map`, `Set`, `RegExp`, `BigInt`, typed arrays, and circular structures all survive.

## Ship the answer, not the computation

When a function is baked, the imports that **only** fed it become dead. Thus `bake` removes them. If `buildNav` came from a server-only module, that import is fully gone from the output. The dependencies of the computation never stay in the graph, and the server guard of Kit has nothing to complain about. Imports still used in other parts of the module stay.

## The contract

`fn` is **self-contained**, exactly like a [Bun macro](https://bun.sh/docs/bundler/macros):

- It can use the **imports** and literals of the module, and `await` freely (`async` functions and `Promise.all` operate).
- It must **not** close over the other local variables of the module. The eval bundle carries the imports, not the surrounding scope. A reference to something that is not imported is a build error.
- It must **not** touch runtime-only modules: `$app/*`, the browser, anything that exists only when the app runs.
- Its result must be **devalue-serializable**. A function, a Promise, or a class instance in the result is a build error that names the fix. Bake makes data.

Each violation surfaces at build, with an `[ogygia]` message that names the file and line. Never a silent no-op.

## Where it fits

`bake` operates in `.ts`/`.js` modules, and inside a `.svelte` `<script>`. It is the general form of the idea that the [loaders](/docs/macros/loaders) apply to content, and that [`code`](/docs/macros/code)/[`md`](/docs/macros/md) apply to snippets: settle at build what cannot change between requests, and ship only the result.
